li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal/country"} "Medals by country"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal/athlete"} "Medals by athlete"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal_table"} "Medal tables"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/participation"} "Participations"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/records"} "Olympic Records"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/age"} "Age records"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/age_at_death"} "Age at death"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/lists"} "Lists"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/today"} "Today"]] "\n" [:li {}] "\n" [:li {:class "divider"}] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics"} "Statistics"]] "\n"] "\n"] "\n\n\n\n\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/feedbacks/new"} "Feedback"]] "\n" [:form {:class "navbar-form navbar-left", :role "search", :action "/athletes/quick_search", :accept-charset "UTF-8", :method "post"} [:input {:name "utf8", :type "hidden", :value "✓", :autocomplete "off"}] [:input {:type "hidden", :name "authenticity_token", :value "U8y49wyyMLkudSqZvPAJ47DIRAJKqwtAIo_UwaLF1_qDStXdWcFPiE2ModOrVgOTt0_ovuXEwaCw1RNq217mrQ", :autocomplete "off"}] [:div {:class "form-group"} "\n" [:input {:type "text", :name "query", :id "query", :class "form-control", :placeholder "Athlete search"}] "\n"] "\n" [:input {:type "submit", :name "commit", :value "Go", :class "btn btn-default", :data-disable-with "Go"}] "\n"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "container"} "\n" [:ol {:class "breadcrumb"} "\n" [:li {} "\n" [:a {:href "/"} "Home"] "\n"] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/editions"} "Games"]] [:li {:class "active"} "1998 Winter Olympics Overview"] "\n"] "\n\n\n" [:h1 {} "1998 Winter Olympics"] "\n" [:h2 {} "Facts"] "\n" [:table {:class "biotable"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Competition type"] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic Games"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Number and Year"] "\n" [:td {} "XVIII / 1998"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Host city"] "\n" [:td {} "\nNagano, Japan\n(" [:a {:href "/venues/editions/46"} "Venues"] ")\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Opening ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n 7 February\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Closing ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n22 February\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Competition dates"] "\n" [:td {} "\n7 – 22 February\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "OCOG"] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/organizations/318"} "Nagano Organizing Committee for the Olympic Winter Games of 1998"]] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Participants"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/counts/edition/46"} "2180"] "\nfrom " [:a {:href "/editions/46/countries"} "72 countries"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Medal events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n68\nin 14 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n"]] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "\nOverview\n\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "It had been 26 years since the Olympic Winter Games had been held in the Orient, those also in Japan " [:a {:href "/editions/39"} "at Sapporo"] " on the northern island of Hokkaido. Nagano had been an “upset” choice over the more favored selections of Salt Lake City and Östersund. But the Japanese typically put on a wonderful show. And it was much quieter in Nagano – there was no " [:a {:href "/athletes/81251"} "Tonya"] " or " [:a {:href "/athletes/81471"} "Nancy"] " for the locust-like media to descend upon."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The Games were severely hampered, however, by the weather of Nagano Prefecture. Snow, rain and fog played havoc with the " [:a {:href "/games/ASK/sports/46"} "alpine skiing schedule"] ", causing the " [:a {:href "/results/1434"} "men’s downhill"] ", one of the feature events of the Games, to be cancelled and re-scheduled four times. Going into the second week of the Olympics, there was some concern that the alpine skiing schedule could not be finished before the Closing Ceremony. One run of the " [:a {:href "/results/438"} "four-man bobsled"] " also had to be omitted because of weather."] "\n\n" [:p {} "A number of new events made their Olympic début in Nagano, probably foremost among these being " [:a {:href "/results/20757"} "women’s ice hockey"] ", which was won by the United States team in a mild upset over the favored Canadians. Snowboarding and curling also were new to the program. Snowboarding had four events – men’s and women’s halfpipe and giant slalom. In the men’s giant slalom, the biggest controversy of the Nagano Olympics occurred when Canadian " [:a {:href "/athletes/99650"} "Ross Rebagliati"] " won the gold medal, only to be disqualified two days later when his doping test came back positive for marijuana. He and the Canadian team appealed to the Court of Arbitration for Sport and Rebagliati was reinstated, and allowed to keep his gold medal."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In " [:a {:href "/results/20713"} "men’s ice hockey"] ", the big story was the presence of the top professional players in the world for the first time ever. The National Hockey League (NHL) closed down its mid-season schedule for two weeks to allow all the pros to represent their countries, reminiscent of the “Dream Team” of NBA players at Barcelona. The difference in ice hockey, however, was that the top players were not solely from one nation, but were spread among several hockey powers – Canada, United States, Russia, Sweden, Finland, and the Czech Republic. Canada looked to restore its lost dominance in the sport by having its pros bring back a gold medal, but it was not to be. The two favorites, the United States and Canada, went out early, and neither won a medal. The final came down to Russia against the Czech Republic, and the Czechs won in a slight upset, aided by the superb goaltending of " [:a {:href "/athletes/86490"} "Dominik Hašek"] " of the Buffalo Sabres of the NHL. Led by Hašek, the Czech team defeated successively the three greatest hockey nations in Olympic history – Canada, Russia, and the United States."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Norway’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/85378"} "Bjørn Dæhlie"] " added to his list of Olympic records by winning four medals and three golds, to bring his overall Olympic total to 12 medals and 8 gold medals, all records for the Olympic Winter Games. Russia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/81634"} "Larisa Lazutina"] " won the most medals at Nagano, with five in women’s nordic skiing. She and Dæhlie both won three gold medals, the only athletes to pull off the trifecta in Nagano."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Also dominant at Nagano were the Dutch speed skaters, whose men won nine of 15 Olympic medals, and four events. " [:a {:href "/athletes/99388"} "Marianne Timmer"] " also added two golds in the women’s " [:a {:href "/results/1104"} "1,000"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/1105"} "1,500"] ". Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/81514"} "Gunda Niemann-Stirnemann"] " won three medals in speed skating, bringing her Olympic career total to eight, equalling the Olympic speed skating record of her countrywoman " [:a {:href "/athletes/81028"} "Karia Kania"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In alpine skiing, " [:a {:href "/athletes/82343"} "Katja Seizinger"] " (GER) won three medals, the third consecutive Olympic Winter Games at which she had won alpine medals, equalling the mark of " [:a {:href "/athletes/98310"} "Alberto Tomba"] " of Italy. Tomba also competed at Nagano, but failed to finish in both the " [:a {:href "/results/1436"} "giant slalom"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/1439"} "slalom"] ", ending his remarkable Olympic career that began in " [:a {:href "/editions/43"} "1988 at Calgary"] "."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Bid process"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "Bid voting at the 97th " [:a {:href "/meetings"} "IOC Session"] " in Birmingham, England on 15 June 1991."] "\n\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped"} "\n  \t" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n  \t\t" [:th {}] "\n  \t\t" [:th {}] "\n  \t\t" [:th {} "Round 1"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "Tiebreak"] "\n  \t\t" [:th {} "Round 2"] "\n  \t\t" [:th {} "Round 3"] "\n  \t\t" [:th {} "Round 4"] "\n  \t"] [:tr {} "\n  \t\t" [:td {:class "city"} "Nagano"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "Japan"] "\n  \t\t" [